# Aurora Term — Rust & project standards

These rules are the project's source of truth. They are mirrored by `agents.md`
and `claude.md` (symlinks) so every agent tool stays in sync.

## Rust coding standards

* Prioritize correctness and clarity; speed/efficiency are secondary unless stated.
* No comments that summarize code. Comments only explain non-obvious **why**.
* Never `unwrap()`/`expect()` in production paths. Propagate errors with `?` into `AppError`.
* Never silently swallow fallible errors. Prefer `?`, `if let Err(e)` handling, or
  `tracing` logging. `let _ =` is only acceptable for intentional best-effort
  cleanup (e.g. killing a child on shutdown).
* Avoid panicking operations (indexing, `.unwrap()`). Guard out-of-bounds work.
* Never create `mod.rs` files — prefer `src/some_module.rs`.
* Use full words for variable names (no `q` for `queue`).
* Use variable shadowing to scope clones in async contexts for clarity.

## Crate separation of concerns (hard rule)

**Each crate has exactly ONE job.** Respect the boundary; do not leak
responsibilities across crates.

* `aurora-core` — shared types, `AppError`, config schema. Zero I/O, zero Tauri, zero async.
* `aurora-pty` — PTY lifecycle, sessions, readers. No Tauri.
* `aurora-db` — SQLite history + search. No Tauri.
* `aurora-config` — JSON + keychain persistence. No Tauri; `new()` takes `PathBuf`, never `&impl Manager`.
* `aurora-sidecar` — sidecar process lifecycle. No Tauri.
* `aurora-lsp-fetch` — prebuilt bundle acquisition (download/verify/extract). No Tauri, no server spawn.
* `aurora-lsp` — language-server process lifecycle only. No Tauri, no network, no downloads.
* `aurora-commands` — thin IPC adapters only. No business logic; delegate to crates.
* `aurora-app` (Tauri binary) — the **only** crate allowed to touch Tauri APIs.

Rules of engagement:
* Route new logic to the crate whose single job covers it. If a change would make
  a crate do two jobs, extend the responsible crate or add a new single-job crate.
* Dependencies flow downward only: `aurora-core` depends on nothing internal;
  feature crates depend on `aurora-core`; `commands`/`app` depend on feature crates.
* Library crates communicate results via `tokio::sync::mpsc` channels, never by
  calling Tauri APIs directly.

## Concurrency & async

* Use `tokio::sync::Mutex`, never `std::sync::Mutex`, for async state.
* Never hold a lock across a slow/await operation (network, process spawn, file I/O).
  Lock briefly, release, do the slow work, re-lock only to commit results.
* Never nest async mutex locks (`tokio::sync::Mutex` is not reentrant) — take, drop,
  then take again.
* Independent work must not block each other. Use per-resource/per-key locks to
  coalesce, and keep global locks out of the hot path (e.g. language-server setup
  runs fully in parallel across different languages).
* Spawn background tasks with `tokio::spawn`; forward results over channels.

## Errors & logging

* `AppError` is the single error type everywhere; each crate converts `Into` it and
  derives `serde::Serialize` so Tauri can serialize it to the frontend.
* Log with `tracing::info!`/`tracing::error!`. Never `println!`.
* Secrets (API keys) live only in the OS keychain (`keyring`) — never in config,
  logs, or the frontend. Frontend receives `hasApiKey: bool` only.
* IDs are `uuid::Uuid::new_v4().to_string()`.

## Hard rules

* Never hardcode model strings or filesystem paths in Rust — read from `AppConfig`
  or resolve via Tauri path APIs.
* Never call a provider/adapter directly from a command — go through its router/trait.
* Never add Tauri deps to any crate except `aurora-app`.
* Never `invoke()` outside `app/src/lib/ipc.ts`; mirror every Rust IPC struct there.
* Run `cargo clippy --workspace -- -D warnings` (zero warnings) and
  `cargo test --workspace` before any commit.

## Pull request hygiene

* Clear, capitalized, imperative title; no conventional-commit prefixes; no trailing punctuation.
* Optionally prefix with the crate name when one crate is the clear scope.
* End the body with a `Release Notes:` section (blank line after) containing
  `- Added / Fixed / Improved ...` or `- N/A`.

## Rules hygiene

* Keep this file high-signal. Prefer "traps to avoid" over "maps to follow";
  architectural layouts go stale — read the code for structure.
* Do not edit `.rules` inline during normal feature/fix work. Propose changes in a
  **"Suggested .rules additions"** section of the PR; reviewers merge them.
* A new rule must be: non-obvious, repeatedly encountered, and specific enough to act on.
